# Cursor Rules: TDD + V-Model Development Practices

## Project Overview
This repository enforces Test-Driven Development (TDD) and V-model development practices with bidirectional traceability. All code changes must be traceable from requirements through design to tests.

## Core Principles

### 1. Requirements-First Development
- Every feature MUST start with a requirement in `docs/requirements/` (REQ-xxxx)
- Requirements MUST be verifiable with explicit verification methods
- Use the YAML format defined in `docs/requirements/requirements-template.yaml`

### 2. Test-Driven Development (TDD)
- Write tests BEFORE implementation code (Red → Green → Refactor)
- Every test MUST reference at least one requirement ID (REQ-xxxx)
- Tests must be deterministic, hermetic, and fast
- Prefer behavioral assertions over implementation details

### 3. Traceability Requirements
- Every REQ-xxxx MUST have at least one design reference in `docs/design/`
- Every REQ-xxxx MUST have at least one test reference in `tests/`
- Design docs MUST include a "Traces to:" section listing requirements
- Test files MUST include REQ-xxxx in comments or docstrings

### 4. V-Model Phase Gates
- Requirements → Design → Implementation → Unit Test → Integration Test → System Test
- Each phase validates the corresponding specification phase
- No phase can be skipped without explicit justification

## ID Conventions (Mandatory)
- Requirements: `REQ-0001`, `REQ-0002`, ...
- Use Cases: `UC-0001`, `UC-0002`, ...
- Design Items: `DES-0001`, `DES-0002`, ...
- Tests (optional): `TST-0001`, `TST-0002`, ...

## File Organization
```
docs/
  product/        # PRDs, user goals (what to build)
  requirements/   # Verifiable requirements (REQ-xxxx) - YAML
  use_cases/      # Use cases (UC-xxxx) - Markdown
  design/         # Design specs (DES-xxxx) - Markdown
  verification/   # Verification plans - YAML
  test_plans/     # Test plans - YAML
  traceability/   # Traceability reports
  reviews/        # Review checklists
  user/           # User-facing documentation

src/              # Production source code
tests/            # All tests (unit/integration/system)
tools/            # Repository tooling
agents/           # Agent prompt playbooks
```

## Agent Selection Guide
When working on this codebase, use the appropriate agent:

| Task | Agent |
|------|-------|
| New feature requirements | `requirements-author.md` |
| Review requirements | `requirements-reviewer.md` |
| Write use cases | `use-case-author.md` |
| Create design doc | `design-author.md` |
| Review design | `cdr-reviewer.md` |
| TDD workflow | `tdd-coach.md` |
| Generate tests | `test-generator.md` |
| Review tests | `test-reviewer.md` |
| Fix traceability | `traceability-manager.md` |
| C++ quality | `cpp-quality-enforcer.md` |
| Python quality | `python-quality-enforcer.md` |
| CI changes | `ci-impact-reviewer.md` |
| Doc updates | `doc-change-manager.md` |
| User doc review | `user-doc-reviewer.md` |

## Before Committing
1. Run traceability check: `python tools/traceability/check_traceability.py --docs docs --tests tests`
2. Run linting: `ruff check . && ruff format --check .`
3. Run tests: `pytest`
4. Ensure all new requirements have design and test coverage

## Code Style
- Python: Follow ruff rules (pyproject.toml)
- C++: Follow cpp-quality-enforcer.md conventions
- All code must be type-annotated (Python) or use modern C++ idioms

## Common Workflows

### Adding a New Feature
1. Create requirement: `docs/requirements/REQ-XXXX-feature-name.yaml`
2. Write use case (if needed): `docs/use_cases/UC-XXXX.md`
3. Create design doc: `docs/design/DES-XXXX-feature-name.md`
4. Write failing tests (TDD): `tests/test_feature.py` (include REQ-xxxx)
5. Implement minimal code to pass tests
6. Refactor while keeping tests green
7. Run traceability check

### Fixing a Bug
1. Identify affected requirement (REQ-xxxx)
2. Write failing test that reproduces the bug
3. Fix the bug (minimal change)
4. Verify all tests pass
5. Update documentation if behavior changed

### Reviewing Code
1. Check traceability (requirement → design → test)
2. Verify tests cover the requirements
3. Run appropriate reviewer agent (CDR, test, requirements)
4. Ensure CI checks pass
